## Mixed-Metal MOF-74 Templated Catalysts for Efficient Carbon Dioxide Capture and Methanation
The vast chemical and structural tunability of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are beginning to be harnessed as functional supports for catalytic nanoparticles spanning a range of applications. However, a lack of straightforward methods for producing nanoparticle-encapsulated MOFs as efficient heterogeneous catalysts limits their usage. Herein, a mixed-metal MOF, NiMg-MOF-74, is utilized as a template to disperse small Ni nanoclusters throughout the parent MOF. By exploiting the difference in \(\mathsf{N i-O}\) and \(\mathsf{M g-O}\) coordination bond strength, \(\mathsf{N i}^{2+}\) is selectively reduced to form highly dispersed Ni nanoclusters constrained by the parent MOF pore diameter, while \(\mathsf{M g}^{2+}\) remains coordinated in the framework. By varying the ratio of Ni to \(\mathsf{M g}\) in the parent MOF, accessible surface area and crystallinity can be tuned upon thermal treatment, influencing \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption capacity and hydrogenation selectivity. The resulting Ni nanoclusters prove to be an active catalyst for \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) methanation and are examined using extended X-ray absorption fine structure and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy. By preserving a segment of the \(\mathsf{M g}^{2+}\)-containing MOF framework, the composite system retains a portion of its \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) adsorption capacity while continuing to deliver catalytic activity. The approach is thus critical for designing materials that can bridge the gap between carbon capture and \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) utilization.
## 1. Introduction
As society transitions away from its reliance on fossil fuels, there remains a need to mitigate rising atmospheric greenhouse gas emissions. Carbon dioxide capture and utilization has gained significant attention in recent years, whereby a catalyst facilitates the conversion of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) toward valuable C1 commodities such as methane \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\right)\), carbon monoxide (CO), formic acid (HCOOH), and methanol \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\right)\).[1] \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) represents the primary component in liquefied natural gas, a well-established energy source compatible with existing infrastructure. \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) is also currently used as feedstock for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production. Converting unwanted \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) into valuable chemical feedstocks such as \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) may help limit the rise in atmospheric \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) concentration.[2]
Metal nanoparticles are effective catalysts for the conversion of carbon dioxide; however, they are prone to sintering and aggregation, which often invokes a loss
in activity. Consequently, it is important to establish a suitable host material to help maintain catalyst dispersion.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline materials constructed from metal ions and organic linkers that form structures with a large internal surface area. Through careful selection of building units during assembly, an array of distinct MOF architectures is possible, each with its own unique compositional and structural properties. The versatility of MOF construction makes this class of porous materials suitable for a range of applications in sorption, separation, sensing, catalysis, and conduction.[3]
Nanoparticles have been introduced into MOFs via several methods including solution infiltration, chemical vapor deposition, and solid grinding.[4] Undesirably, these approaches often lead to large nanoparticles forming on the exterior of the MOF framework due to incomplete infiltration of the metal precursors, inhibiting their usefulness as a catalyst.[5] Atomic layer deposition has been used to control the formation of nanoparticles within MOF pores.[6] However, this approach requires specialized equipment and expensive volatile metal precursors. Alternately, MOFs have been grown directly on metallic nanoparticles, leading to core–shell structures. However, the presence of metallic nanoparticles has been shown to disturb MOF self-assembly, invoking excessive defect sites in the MOFs
and strongly impacting their stability. In addition, effectual implementation of the external growth approach is largely confined to ZIF-8 and UiO-66 MOF architectures, limiting its use more broadly. In situations where metal nanoparticles have been successfully included in MOF pores, the resulting composite materials have been successfully applied to hydrogenation reactions, highlighting the implicit benefit of preserving the MOF architecture for catalysis.[4b,7] However, the formation of these composite structures often requires cumbersome preparation techniques, limiting their translation into industrially relevant catalytic processes.
Recently, MOFs have proven useful as sacrificial templates to produce carbonized nanomaterials with unique morphologies.[8] Typically, MOFs are thermally decomposed to form metallic nanoparticles dispersed through carbon and/or metal oxide frameworks. The resulting carbon-based materials have been used as catalysts for a range of reactions, including the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and \(C O_{2}\) reduction.[8a,d,9] For instance, Ni-MOF-74 and Mg-MOF-74 were thermally decomposed to form Ni and \(\mathrm{MgO}\) nanoparticles dispersed through carbonized frameworks, respectively.[10] Thermally treated Ni-MOF-74 has been investigated for the hydrogenation of olefins, with higher pretreatment temperatures \((>\!400\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) leading to greater Ni nanoparticle agglomeration and reduced catalytic activity.[11] While templating leads to a range of unique catalyst morphologies and compositions, the highly ordered microporosity intrinsic to many MOFs is effectively lost in the carbonization process. It would be advantageous if a greater portion of the MOF framework architecture could be preserved upon thermal treatment so that the resulting catalyst could benefit from the framework's porosity, with the foreseeable ability to concentrate reactants, size exclude particulates and larger molecules while maintaining metal catalyst dispersion.
Here, we present a straightforward, controlled approach to construct a Mg-MOF-based scaffold with Ni nanoclusters well-dispersed throughout the framework, utilizing a multimetal MOF (MM–MOF) as the sole template. By exploiting the difference in thermal stability of \(\mathrm{Ni}{-}\mathrm{O}\) and \(\mathrm{Mg}{-}\mathrm{O}\) coordination bonds in NiMg-MOF-74, selective extraction of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) from, and its subsequent clustering in, the MM–MOF scaffold is realized. NiMg-MOF-74 was selected due to the well-documented \(C O_{2}\)
adsorption capacity of Mg-MOF-74, while Ni is known to be catalytically active for \(C O_{2}\) methanation.[12] The findings reveal that the MM-MOF templating approach can produce a Mg-MOF-74 scaffold decorated with catalytically active Ni nanoclusters as a heterogeneous catalyst for the simultaneous capture and conversion of \(C O_{2}\) . The capacity for using the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\mathrm{:}\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) ratio to regulate Ni nanoparticle formation, MOF structural stability and catalytic activity/selectivity, is also demonstrated. Materials such as these signify the potential for nanoparticles embedded within MOFs for both carbon capture and carbon utilization, bridging the two applications and allowing \(C O_{2}\) emissions in the atmosphere to be efficiently managed.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Synthesis and Selective Thermal Decomposition of NiMg-MOF-74
MOF-74 (also known as \(\mathrm{M}_{2}\mathrm{DOBDC}\) and CPO-27-M), comprised of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) and \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) nodes, was synthesized with a range of relative Ni to \(\mathrm{Mg}\) atomic compositions (namely \(100\mathrm{Mg}\) , \(20\mathrm{Ni}80\mathrm{Mg}\) , \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\) , \(60\mathrm{Ni}40\mathrm{Mg}\) , \(80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg}\) , and \(100\mathrm{Ni})\) to assess the extent of Ni nanoparticle formation and MOF structural integrity upon thermal treatment ( \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\,\mathrm{h}\) ). Inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy (ICP–OES) analysis indicated that the experimental ratio of \(\mathrm{Ni}{\mathrm{:}}\mathrm{Mg}\) deviates from what was used in the synthesis, with \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) species slightly more abundant in the resulting MOF architecture relative to the quantity used during synthesis (Figure S1, Supporting Information). Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the neat MOF-74 materials (Figure 1; and Figure S2, Supporting Information) closely matched the simulated peaks for MOF-74, while the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption/ desorption isotherms (Figure S3, Supporting Information) revealed the presence of micropores (Figure S4, Supporting Information), consistent with what has been reported in the literature.[13] Calculated Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas (Figure 1b) resembled the theoretically accessible surface area of both Mg-MOF-74 \((1776\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and Ni-MOF-74 \((1356\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) , indicating effective solvent evacuation.[14]
As the quantity of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) increases in the framework, peak broadening was observed in the neat MOF-74 XRD profiles, most notably for the intense reflections at low angles. Rather than a lack of crystallinity, the source of peak broadening is likely due to the small MOF crystallite size of \(80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg}\) and 100Ni (Figures S5–S10, Supporting Information). Similar XRD peak broadening and nanocrystallite aggregation has been seen elsewhere for MOF-74 nanocrystals.[13,15]
MOF-74 predominantly comprising \({\mathrm{Mg}}^{2+}\) (100Mg, 20Ni80Mg, and \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\)) retained their crystallinity and accessible surface area more readily than \(60\mathrm{Ni}40\mathrm{Mg}\), \(80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg}\), and 100Ni upon thermal treatment (Figure 1a; Figure S2 (Supporting Information) and Figure 1b; Figure S3 (Supporting Information), respectively). In the case of 100Ni, a complete loss of MOF crystallinity was observed upon treatment at \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure 1a), coinciding with the emergence of face centered cubic (fcc) Ni peaks. A partial decomposition was evident in the XRD diffractograms of \(60\mathrm{Ni}40\mathrm{Mg}\) and \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\), with reflections for MOF-74 being retained as fcc-Ni reflections emerge.
The trend in thermal stability with varying \(\mathrm{Ni:Mg}\) ratios as exhibited by the XRD profiles is also reflected in the thermogravimetric analysis results (Figure S11, Supporting Information). The thermodynamic stability of MOFs typically relies on the node-linker coordination bond strength.[16] The junction between the secondary building unit and linker can be understood as a Lewis acid/base pair where the strength of the bond
is dictated by the acidity of the metal ion and the basicity of the deprotonated linker.[17] Considering that \(\mathrm{Mg}\) is a stronger acid than Ni, this may account for the enhanced thermal stability of Mg-MOF-74 over Ni-MOF-74.
Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) was also used to assess the linker stability of NiMg-MOF-74 under elevated temperatures (Figure 2a,b; and S12, Supporting Information). FTIR spectra suggested that the 2,3-dioxo-1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (DOBDC) linker integrity was preserved for frameworks comprised primarily of \(\mathrm{Mg}\) ions (100Mg, 20Ni80Mg, and \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\)) when treated at \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). For materials with higher Ni content (60Ni40Mg, 80Ni20Mg, and 100Ni), a diminished absorbance was observed, indicating linker decomposition. The assignment of vibrational bands for \(\mathrm{Mg}\) and Ni-MOF-74 can be found in Table S5 (Supporting Information).
Raman spectroscopy provides a complementary approach to probing linker stability. The spectra for pristine MOF-74 matched closely to the vibrational bands of DOBDC seen elsewhere (Figure 2c).[18] As the \(\mathrm{Ni:Mg}\) ratio was increased, framework carbonization was increasingly evident after thermal treatment (Figure S13, Supporting Information). For samples containing minimal Ni (100Mg and 20Ni80Mg), negligible graphitic carbon formation was observed in the Raman spectra. For \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\), peaks associated with the disorder-induced D band \((1350\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) and \(\mathrm{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}\) stretching G band \((1580\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})\) of graphitic carbon began to emerge, while peaks associated with
the parent framework were partially preserved indicating a composite MOF/carbon structure. As the Ni content was further increased (60Ni40Mg, 80Ni20Mg, and 100Ni), the graphitic carbon peaks dominated the spectra, while peaks associated with the parent MOF disappeared, indicating extensive framework carbonization. It is worthwhile noting that for samples containing no \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) (i.e., pure Mg-MOF-74), linker decomposition was not observed from FTIR or Raman spectra at \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
## 2.2. Evidence of Nickel Nanoparticle Formation
Transmission electron microscopy was conducted to gain an insight into how \(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) content influenced nanoparticle formation within NiMg-MOF-74 (Figure 3). Upon thermal treatment at \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), the MOF particles became speckled with higher contrast domains, indicating nanoparticle formation. Unfortunately, due to the electron beam sensitivity of MOFs, we were unable to probe the structure at higher resolutions and were limited to operating at \(100\,\mathrm{kV}\) e-beam energy. Examples of sample damage when operating at \(200\,\mathrm{kV}\) e-beam energy can be found in Figures S14–S16 (Supporting Information). While it is difficult to quantify the size of these dark spots accurately, they appear to be \(<5\,\mathrm{nm}\) in diameter. The presence of these high-contrast regions was more obvious for the 40Ni60Mg, 60Ni40Mg, 80Ni20Mg, and 100Ni samples (Figures S7–S10, Supporting Information). While it was difficult to view similar regions in the samples containing larger MOF particles due to sample thickness, they were visible near the exterior of the particles for 20Ni80Mg (Figure S6, Supporting Information). No noticeable change in 100Mg was observable (Figure S5, Supporting Information), although changes in particle structure may be obscured by sample thickness.
