Mukoyoshi et al. proposed that the 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid linker was able to facilitate the reduction of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) by cleaving the \(\mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{O}\) bond in Ni-MOF-74 and generating a semiquinone-like radical akin to other quinone-based metal complexes.[10a] Once generated, the radical intermediate was able to promote further reduction of neighboring Ni ions prior to forming a stable quinone. This mechanism may explain the generation of metal nanoparticles upon the pretreatment of MOF-74 under vacuum in the absence of a \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) flow.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was also used to gain insights into how NiMg-MOF-74 changed upon thermal treatment (Figure 4). Spin–orbit splitting peaks in the Ni 2p region for NiMg-MOF-74 were observed centered at binding energies of 855.7 \((2\mathrm{p}_{3/2})\) and 873.0 eV \((2\mathrm{p}_{1/2})\), corresponding to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) coordination bonds in NiMg-MOF-74 (Figure S17, Supporting Information). Upon thermal treatment at \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), a new pair of spin–orbit splitting peaks were observed at binding energies of 853.2 \((2\mathrm{p}_{3/2})\) and 870.7 eV \((2\mathrm{p}_{1/2})\), indicating the formation of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{0}\). As the Ni content in NiMg-MOF-74 increased, the peaks associated with \(\mathrm{Ni}^{0}\) increased in magnitude, suggesting further decomposition of the framework and release of the Ni, which is consistent with our other observations. The complexity of the Ni 2p peak shapes due to the presence of a variety of structural loss contributions from plasmon effects, shake-up, and multiplet splitting makes it difficult to quantify the ratio of \(\mathrm{Ni}^{0}\) to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) in the pretreated samples.[19] High resolution XPS spectra for the Mg 1s, C 1s, and O 1s regions can be found in Figures S18–S20 (Supporting Information), along with survey spectra (Figures S18–S22, Supporting Information).
The Mg oxidation state and coordination environment after thermal treatment is also probed using XPS (Figure S18, Supporting Information). A shift of \(+1\ \mathrm{eV}\) after treatment indicates a slight decrease in electron density, possibly due to the removal of preadsorbed species at the \(\mathrm{Mg}\) open metal sites upon thermal treatment. No significant change in peak shape is observed, suggesting that the \(\mathrm{Mg}\) coordination environment is largely retained throughout the treatment process. Near edge X-ray absorption fine structure (NEXAFS) spectra of the \(\mathrm{Mg}\) K-edge, which provide coordination-dependent details of the local \(\mathrm{Mg}\) structure and chemistry, further corroborate the XPS results (Figure S23, Supporting Information). No evolution of peak shape is observed in all MOF materials after thermal treatment, indicating a consistent \(\mathrm{Mg}\) coordination environment. Furthermore, the data poses little-to-no resemblance to NEXAFS spectra in the literature for \(\mathrm{MgO}\) or \(\mathrm{Mg}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\), suggesting that these species are not present in the thermally treated materials.[20]
To probe the effect of thermal treatment more precisely on the Ni present in NiMg-MOF-74, X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) was performed to resolve local structural changes within the MOF structure. The X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra, shown in Figure 5, provide information on the Ni oxidation state and local structure of the Ni in NiMg-MOF-74 before and after thermal treatment.
Prior to thermal decomposition, the XANES exhibit clear trends in both white line intensity as a function of Ni composition. The white line intensity is higher for samples with increasing amounts of \(\mathrm{Mg}\), indicating a strong influence of \(\mathrm{Mg}\) on the linker chemistry to polarize electron density away from the Ni atoms. The decrease in white line intensity is particularly notable in \(80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg}\) and \(100\mathrm{Ni}\) where the influence on \(\mathrm{Mg}\) chemistry is minimal in the former and absence in the later. Note that \(\mathrm{E}_{0}\) changes minimally with \(\mathrm{Mg}\) composition, signifying that the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) oxidation state is maintained. Upon thermal treatment, the XANES changes away from the initial MOF-based structure, indicating a conversion of the Ni nodes into Ni nanoclusters. When compared to a reference Ni foil, it is clear that the majority of Ni atoms appear to become oxidized after thermal treatment and are more resemblant of NiO or \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\). As expected, XANES from all NiMg-MOF-74 samples reveal a shift to lower edge energy \((\mathrm{E}_{0})\) consistent with a partial \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) reduction to \(\mathrm{Ni}^{0}\) (Figure S24, Supporting Information).
To better understand the resulting composition of the Ni nanoclusters post thermal treatment, linear combination fitting (LCF) of the XANES was performed to reveal the ratios of Ni, NiO, and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) were present in the samples (Figure S25 and Table S8, Supporting Information). \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) and NiO accounted for more than \(65\%\) of all the Ni present within each
of the thermally treated samples. However, a portion of Ni remained in a metallic state, with \(12\%\pm1\), \(11\%\pm1\), \(34\%\pm1\), \(23\%\pm6\), and \(10\%\pm8\) observed in \(20\mathrm{Ni}80\mathrm{Mg}\) 350, \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\) 350, \(60\mathrm{Ni}40\mathrm{Mg}\) 350, \(80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg}\) 350, and \(100\mathrm{Ni}\) \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), respectively. The remaining contributions from the LCF are from NiO and \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\), wherein the majority of the XANES is best fit with a large \(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\) contribution (see Table S8, Supporting Information).
The extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) data are obtained by converting the XAS data to \(k\)-space \((\mathrm{k}^{2}\)-weighted) followed by a Fourier transformation into \(r\)-space and modeled to obtain coordination numbers (CNs) and interatomic distances. The main features observed for the neat materials are consistent with what is expected for MOF-74 (Figure S26, Supporting Information). Each Ni species is coordinated with five oxygen species in a square pyramidal geometry with the sixth coordination site vacated upon solvent evacuation. The ratio of Ni-Ni and \(\mathrm{Ni-Mg}\) within the local Ni coordination environment is consistent with ICP-OES results (Figure S1 and Table S1, Supporting Information), suggesting the Ni and \(\mathrm{Mg}\) species are randomly distributed throughout the framework as seen elsewhere, rather than regions of discrete Ni-MOF-74 and Mg-MOF-74.[21] The Ni-C backscattering contribution is slightly overrepresented when compared with the theoretical structure which could be due to the similarity in interatomic distances between Ni-O and Ni-C. The neat MOF EXAFS modeling fits (Figures S27-S31, Supporting Information) along with the resulting fitting parameters (Table S9, Supporting Information).
