The effective coordination number from EXAFS measurements can be used to evaluate the resulting nanoparticle size, particularly for particle diameters below \(3\;\mathrm{nm}\).[22] For each filled concentric shell of Ni, there is an average coordination number across all atoms within the particle. Using the unit cell size of fcc-Ni \((0.352\ \mathrm{nm})\) and recognizing that fcc metals such as Ni tend to form cuboctahedron geometries, it is possible to calculate the side length of the particle. With the side length, a corresponding diameter of volume-equivalent sphere can be calculated for the given cuboctahedra. A similar approach can be used for nanoclusters of other geometries, such as icosahedron. For more details, see Figures S37 and S38 in the Supporting Information. From this analysis, the resulting nanoparticle diameters are \(1.0\pm0.2\), \(0.7\pm0.1\), \(2.8\pm0.2\), \(1.0\pm0.2\), and \(0.9\pm0.2\ \mathrm{nm}\) for \(20\mathrm{Ni}80\mathrm{Mg}\), \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\), \(60\mathrm{Ni}40\mathrm{Mg}\), \(80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg}\), and \(100\mathrm{Ni}\), respectively. All of the nanoparticle sizes, except for \(60\mathrm{Ni}40\mathrm{Mg}\), are smaller than the pore size of the parent MOF \((1.1\ \mathrm{nm})\) implying that the original MOF geometry constrains particle growth once thermally treated.
## 2.3. Carbon Dioxide Capture and Utilization Using NiMg-MOF-74
The \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity of NiMg-MOF-74 at \(273\ \mathrm{K}\) was studied before and after thermal treatment (Figure 7a,7b). As more \(\mathrm{Mg}\) is included in the MOF, an increase in \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) adsorption capacity was observed, demonstrating the importance of \(\mathrm{Mg}\) open metal sites in MOF-74 for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) capture.
The \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) adsorption capacity of \( 100\mathrm{Ni} \) is lower than what has been seen elsewhere.[12a] It is possible that the Ni open metal sites critical to \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) adsorption have undergone a subtle change in local coordination environment due to the lengthy pretreatment \( 250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \) under vacuum) required to completely evacuate the Mg-portion of the framework. The thermal treatment used has not affected the \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) adsorption capacity of \( 100\mathrm{Ni} \) (Figure 1), implying that pore blockage or incomplete evacuation are not responsible for the lower than expected \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) uptake.
As seen with the \( \mathrm{N}_{2} \) adsorption/desorption isotherms, thermal treatment impacts the uptake of \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) due to partial framework decomposition. As more Ni is included in the MOF, a greater loss in \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) adsorption capacity was observed (Figure 7c). \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) capacity was more readily retained for \( 100\mathrm{Mg} \) and \( 20\mathrm{Ni}80\mathrm{Mg} \) samples with relatively minor losses in \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) uptake of \( 6.2\% \) and \( 11.4\% \), respectively, at \( 105\,\mathrm{kPa} \). A significant loss in microporosity for \( 80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg} \) and \( 100\mathrm{Ni} \) after thermal
treatment at \( 350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) (Figure S4 and Table S3, Supporting Information) resulted in \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) capacity decreases of \( 84.1\% \) and \( 85.7\% \), respectively. Both \( 40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg} \) and \( 60\mathrm{Ni}40\mathrm{Mg} \) lost \( 28.8\% \) and \( 29.1\% \) of their \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) adsorption capacity upon thermal treatment. The diminished \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) uptake of MOF-74 comprised of predominantly Ni species highlights the role of increased \( \mathrm{Mg} \) content on framework stability for \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) capture.
The catalytic activity of NiMg-MOF-74 for \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) methanation was assessed over a range \( (250{-}350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}) \) of reaction temperatures (Figure 8). As the temperature was elevated to \( 350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) conversion increased for all samples. Selectivity varied significantly for each catalyst, with samples comprised of more Ni \( (80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg} \) and \( 100\mathrm{Ni}) \) possessing greater selectivity toward CO, while those containing more \( \mathrm{Mg} \) \( (20\mathrm{Ni}80\mathrm{Mg} \), \( 40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg} \), and \( 60\mathrm{Ni}40\mathrm{Mg}) \) were selective \( (>90\%) \) toward \( \mathrm{CH}_{4} \) at \( 350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). \( 40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg} \) achieved the highest conversion \( (62\%) \), indicating that it was the most suitable material for \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) methanation.
In general, the selectivity toward methane increases with increasing temperature for all catalysts. At Ni:Mg ratios greater than 80:20, there is a distinct shift toward CO selectivity overall, irrespective of reaction temperature. For \( 80\,\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg} \) and \( 100\,\mathrm{Ni} \), selectivity results at \( 250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) were not reported as the low conversions \( (<\!5\%) \) meant that quantifying the products was unreliable. For cases where the Ni:Mg ratios are less than 60:40, the formation of methane is favored.
The Ni oxidation state plays an important role in defining catalyst performance during the methanation reaction. Metallic Ni is a known active site responsible for facilitating the \( \mathsf{C O}_{2} \) methanation reaction.[23] The reducibility of Ni is an important feature of catalyst performance in this context.[24] The presence of oxidised Ni species (such as NiO and \( \mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2} \)) due to insufficient pretreatment has been shown to result in diminished methanation activity.[25]
Throughout performance testing, there is the potential for \( \mathrm{NiO/Ni(OH)}_{2} \) to be present in the catalyst. The existence of \( \mathrm{NiO}/ \) \( \mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OH})_{2} \) may arise from: i) incomplete reduction of the Ni species during the MOF thermal treatment process ( \( 350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for \( 12\,\mathrm{h} \) under vacuum); or ii) passivation of the Ni NP/cluster surface during transfer from the pretreatment unit to the quartz reactor. In the former case, the only oxygen source would be from the decomposing MOF linkers whereby some of the Ni may not achieve a completely reduced form if the "O" was not completely decoupled from the Ni by the vacuum. The latter case seems to be more likely as Ni nanoclusters can readily undergo passivation when exposed to air. Passivated Ni can be readily re-reduced at a much lower temperature than bulk NiO as was demonstrated in a study by Jantarang et al.[24] They observed that their passivated Ni (loaded on \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \)) was able to be reduced below \( 200\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) compared to \( 400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for "bulk NiO." Given that the methanation
experiments are performed at between 250 and \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under a \(30\;\mathrm{vol}\%\) \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) flow it is anticipated that any Ni passivation will be removed during the reaction.
To understand how pretreatment time influences catalytic activity, a series of performance tests were conducted targeting the best performing catalyst \((40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg})\) upon vacuum pretreatment at \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 3, 6, 12, and \(24~\mathrm{h}\) (Figure S39, Supporting Information). When lowering (3, 6 h) and raising (24 h) the initial pretreatment duration \((12\;\mathrm{h})\) , there appears to only be a slight change in both \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) selectivity. The variation in performance fell within the error bars of our initial performance results (Figure 8) as calculated from three independent experiments of \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\) thermally treated at \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12~\mathrm{h}\) . All \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\) pretreated materials are similar in composition (Table S11, Supporting Information), suggesting that most of the MOF decomposition occurs sometime within the first \(3\,\mathrm{h}\) of thermal treatment. The similarity in catalyst composition (in particular Ni loading) helps explain why the catalytic performance (Figure S39, Supporting Information) appears independent of pre-treatment durations examined (3, 6, 12, and \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) ) for \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\) .
A control experiment was conducted to understand whether the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) open metal sites present in MOF-74 are catalytically active for the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) methanation reaction (Figure S40, Supporting Information). The best performing material \((40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg})\) was degassed at \(250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (avoiding the formation of Ni nanoclusters) and performance tested at both 200 and \(250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under identical conditions to those used in Figure 8. Neither \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion nor \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) generation was observed, indicating that the Ni open metal sites within Ni-MOF-74 are not the active sites responsible for facilitating the \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) methanation reaction.
Catalyst stability under reaction conditions was also investigated over a \(12\,\mathrm{~h~}\) span. \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion for each catalyst remained relatively stable, with \(60\mathrm{Ni}40\mathrm{Mg}\) and \(80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg}\) experiencing the largest decrease in activity of \(20\%\) and \(10\%\) , respectively. Methane selectivity remained constant for all catalysts over the time scale examined. \(40\mathrm{Ni}60\mathrm{Mg}\) maintained a \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) conversion of close to \(60\%\) throughout the run, undergoing a slight decrease to \(54\%\) after \(12~\mathrm{h}\) . 100Ni retained a low conversion of \(27\%\) over the entire performance test. The relative instability of \(60\mathrm{Ni}40\mathrm{Mg}\) and \(80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg}\) may be due to insufficient \(\mathrm{Mg}\) atoms for MOF coordination leading to a significantly diminished catalyst scaffold after thermal treatment. A similar decrease in performance was not observed for 100Ni since the MOF has already been completely carbonized during thermal treatment. Additional loss in MOF crystallinity is observed for the spent catalysts, supporting this idea (Figures S41 and S42, Supporting Information).
The stark difference in selectivity between the catalysts is an unexpected feature of the performance testing results. To understand what material properties are facilitating the change in selectivity, it is worthwhile considering the various reaction pathways available for CO and \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) formation, as discussed below.
There are two hydrogenation reactions that may take place in the presence of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) , namely the Sabatier reaction 1) and the reverse water–gas shift (RWGS) reaction 2), as detailed in Table 1. The CO produced during the RWGS reaction may also be further hydrogenated toward methane via a six-electron CO methanation pathway 3).
\(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) formation occurs either through the direct hydrogenation of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) or via a CO intermediate.[1d] There is also evidence that formate surface species may form under reaction conditions, however in heterogeneous thermal catalysis it is thought to be a spectator intermediate due to the cumbersome energy barrier to both hydrogenation and dehydrogenation.[1d,26] In photothermal catalytic hydrogenation, formate species appear to overcome these energetic barriers rendering it the dominant reaction pathway particularly at low temperatures \((<\!300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) . For this reason, formate is considered in this discussion.
The partial preservation of the MOF under reaction conditions is influential in facilitating \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) production (Figure 9). For catalysts containing the most Ni \((80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg}\) and 100Ni), there is a selectivity trend toward CO production. Such selectivity is seldom observed in conventional nickel/metal oxide catalysts for \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation.[1c] One possibility is that the encapsulation of Ni by graphitic carbon results in a localized hydrogen deficient environment, unable to facilitate further reduction of the adsorbed CO intermediate. Alternatively, once the adsorbed \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}{}^{-}\) is dissociated on the Ni surface, the change in the metal's electronic state—induced by the carbon shell—results in desorption of the CO species. In either case, the role of the carbon support appears critical to this dramatic shift in selectivity from \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\) to CO.
For \(80\mathrm{Ni}20\mathrm{Mg}\) and 100Ni, much of the MOF architecture has been transformed into carbon-encapsulated Ni upon thermal treatment at \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , analogous to many \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) catalyst materials. While the mechanistic pathways for electrocatalytic \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}\) and thermal \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) hydrogenation differ, the influence that a carbon shell can have on reaction selectivity is notable.
## 3. Conclusion
We have presented a straightforward approach to generating MOF-encapsulated nanoparticle catalysts with a high degree
of control that can be used to both capture and convert \({\mathsf{C O}}_{2}\) into \({\mathsf{C H}}_{4}\) . Due to the difference in coordination bond strength formed between \(\mathsf{N i-O}\) and \(\mathsf{M g-O}\) in NiMg-MOF-74, thermal treatment at \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) resulted in the gradual release of Ni nanoclusters, while preserving the Mg-MOF-74 scaffold. As the amount of \(\mathrm{Mg}\) was increased in the parent framework, accessible surface area and MOF crystallinity were retained, while the formation of graphitic carbon was minimized upon thermal treatment. Interestingly, catalyst performance did not directly correlate with Ni loading, alluding to the ability of Mg-MOF-74 to maintain Ni dispersion. While Ni is known to favor methane formation for the \({\mathsf{C O}}_{2}\) hydrogenation reaction, we uncovered that selectivity could be altered to produce CO once the Ni was engulfed with graphitic carbon. The work demonstrates the potential mixed-metal MOFs possess to bridge the gap between carbon capture and conversion. By leveraging the inherent versatility of MOF construction, a variety of mixed metal MOFs can be used as templates for the formation of highly dispersed nanoparticles throughout the parent framework broadening their effectiveness across a suite of applications including air purification, heterogeneous catalysis, sensing, and energy storage.
## 4. Experimental Section
Synthesis: MOF-74 synthesis was adapted from the literature.[13] In each case, an aqueous solution of metal salt (nickel acetate tetrahydrate and magnesium nitrate hexahydrate) was combined slowly with an aqueous solution of 2,1-dihydroxyterephthalic acid and sodium hydroxide and stirred at room temperature for \(24\ h\) . The molar ratio of metal salt:linker:base was maintained as 2:1:4 in \(200\,\mathrm{\mL}\) of MilliQ water. The prepared suspension was then washed three times with water and methanol to remove unreacted reagents. The washed suspension was solvent exchanged with methanol 12 times, allowing the MOF suspension to mix for 1 day on each occasion to remove any water bound within the MOF pores. To obtain a range of Ni:Mg molar ratios, the initial concentration of metal precursors were adjusted while maintaining a total metal salt concentration \((0.273\,\mathrm{~M})\) with respect to the total reaction volume.
Powder X-Ray Diffraction: X-ray diffraction was performed on a PANalytical Xpert Multipurpose X-ray Diffraction System (MPD) operating at \(45\ \mathrm{~kV}\) and \(40~\mathrm{\mA}\) using a Cu Kα radiation source \((\lambda\,=\,1.5406)\) . Diffraction patterns were collected over a \(5^{\circ}{-}110^{\circ}\ 2\theta\) range with a step size of \(0.026^{\circ}\) acquiring each step for \(60~\mathsf{s}\) . A sample holder ( \(15\,\mathsf{m m}\) diameter, \(1\,\mathsf{\,m m}\) depth) containing powdered MOF-74 was sideloaded to ensure similar particle packing densities between samples. The incident beam path was fitted with a \(1/2^{\circ}\) programmable divergence slit, 1° anticattering slit, and \(15\,\mathsf{m m}\) mask. The diffracted beam was equipped with a \(1/2^{\circ}\) anticattering slit. 0.04 rad Soller slits were also installed. The sample was rotated at 0.25 rev \(\mathsf{s}^{-1}\) to reduce the potential influence of preferred orientation. Diffractograms were analyzed and processed using Highscore Plus software.
Adsorption/Desorption Isotherms: \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and \({\mathsf{C O}}_{2}\) adsorption/desorption isotherms were collected using a Micromeritics TriStar II PLUS instrument at \(-196\) and \(0~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}\) , respectively. Solvent exchanged MOF-74 samples were thermally treated for \(12\,\mathrm{~h~}\) at \(250\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) under vacuum \((<\!0.015\,\mathsf{\,k P a})\) using a Micromeritics Smart VacPrep unit to remove any solvent remaining in the framework micropores.
Attenuated Total Reflection–Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy: Infrared spectra were collected on a Perkin Elmer Spectrum Two Fourier transform infrared spectrometer fitted with an attenuated total reflectance accessory comprised of a single reflection diamond crystal. A wavenumber range spanning \(4000{-}450~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) was recorded with a \(4\,\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) resolution. Each measurement was accumulated for 8 scans.
Raman Spectroscopy: Raman spectra were recorded using a Renishaw InVia Raman microscope equipped with a \(532\;\mathsf{n m}\) wavelength laser over a wavenumber range of \(1800{-}400\;\mathsf{c m}^{-1}\) . Laser output was reduced to \(1\%\) to avoid sample decomposition induced by laser heating
X-Ray Photoelectron Spectroscopy: X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy was conducted on a Thermo-Fischer ESCALAB250i high-resolution X-ray photoelectron spectrometer equipped with a monochromatic Al Kα soft X-ray source \((1486.6\ \mathrm{eV})\) . Survey spectra were collected up to a binding energy of \(1350~\mathrm{eV}\) and data analyzed using CasaXPS. Binding energy values were referenced to the C 1s peak at \(284.8~\mathrm{eV}\) to account for sample charging. The background was subtracted using a Shirley (Integral) procedure within the software prior to peak quantification.
Thermogravimetric Analysis: Thermogravimetric analysis was performed using a TA instruments TGA Q5000. Approximately \(10\,\mathrm{\mg}\) of sample was loaded onto a platinum pan prior to measurement. A heating rate of \(5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}\) under nitrogen flow from room temperature up to \(800~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) was used.
Transmission Electron Microscopy: Transmission electron microscopy was conducted using a JEOL1400 instrument. A low beam voltage \((100\,\,\mathsf{k V})\) and current \((55\ \upmu\mathsf{A})\) were selected to avoid sample damage upon imaging. Samples were dispersed in methanol and drop cast onto carbon grids prior to imaging. Higher resolution transmission electron microscopy was performed using a FEI Tecnai G2 20 TEM operating at \(200~\mathsf{k V}.\) Samples were dispersed in methanol and drop cast onto carbon grids prior to imaging.
Extended X-Ray Absorption Fine Structure Spectroscopy: Ni K-edge XAS measurements were performed at the 10-ID-B beamline of the Advanced Photon Source, Argonne National Laboratory. Samples were loaded into 1 mm OD Kapton capillaries and measured in a transmission geometry, collecting XAS data from \(200~\mathsf{e V}\) below the Ni K-edge to \(800~\mathsf{e V}\) past the edge. All data processing and subsequent analysis was performed using the Demeter XAS software package.[31] EXAFS modeling of the as synthesized Ni/Mg MOFs was performed using the known MOF structure for Ni MOF 74.[32] Ni O, Ni Ni, Ni Mg, and Ni C scattering paths were included in the fitting. The coordination numbers related to Ni Ni and Ni Mg were constrained according to the stoichiometry of the two elements obtained from ICP-OES. Ni nanoparticles from thermally treated MOFs were modeled with known Ni Ni and Ni O scattering contributions from NiO and fcc Ni. A \(S_{0}{}^{2}\) value of 0.78976 was used in all EXAFS models and obtained from modeling a reference Ni foil.
Near-Edge Extended X-Ray Absorption Fine Structure Spectroscopy: Mg K-edge NEXAFS measurements were performed on the fast NEXAFS chamber of the SXR beamline, Australian Synchrotron. MOF powders were mounted onto Cu tape for PEY measurements, where the energy was calibrated with a reference Mg foil upstream from the sample chamber. All data processing and analysis was done in Igor Pro using the QANT NEXAFS tool.[33]
\(C O_{2}\) Hydrogenation Catalytic Experiments: Catalytic activity, selectivity, and stability tests were accomplished using \(50~\mathsf{m g}\) of sample within a quartz reactor \((30\,\mathsf{m m}\) long, \(6\,\mathsf{m m}\) ID). The catalysts were pretreated using the Micromeritics Smart VacPrep unit at \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\,\mathrm{h}\) under vacuum \((<0.015\mathrm{~kPa})\) prior to performance testing.
To examine catalytic activity, light-off profiles for each catalyst were obtained. A reactant gas mixture consisting of \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\;\mathrm{min}^{-1}\;\mathsf{N}_{2}\) (Coregas, \(>\!99.999\%)\) , \(6\ m{\mathsf{L}}\ m{\mathsf{i}}{\mathsf{n}}^{-1}\subset\mathsf{O}_{2}\) (Coregas, \(>\!99.99\%)\) , and \(24\ m{\mathsf{L}}\ m{\mathsf{i}}{\mathsf{n}}^{-1}\ H_{2}\) (Coregas, \(>\!99.999\%)\) for a total flow rate of \(80\ m{\mathsf{L}}\ m{\mathsf{i}}{\mathsf{n}}^{-1}\) (WHSV of \(48\ L\ h^{-1}\ g_{\mathsf{c a t}}{}^{-1})\) was fed to the reactor. A temperature program controller was used to heat the reactor system from 200 to \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at a rate of \(5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) \(\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}\) with a hold step of \(0.5\,\mathfrak{h}\) at each \(50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) increment.
The stability tests were performed using the same catalyst amount, pretreatment and reactant flow rates as the light-off experiments. The temperature was held constant at \(350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(12\,\mathfrak{h}\) .
The gases exiting the reactor during the performance tests were analyzed by two gas chromatographs; a Shimadzu 8A equipped with a thermal conductivity detector and Zeolite 13X column for \(\mathsf{N}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\) detection and a Shimadzu 2010 with a methanizer/flame ionization detector array and HP/Plot Q column for \({\mathsf{C H}}_{4},\) \(\mathsf{C O}_{2},\) and CO detection. The flow rate of the gases exiting the reactor was determined and the subsequent conversion of \(\mathsf{C O}_{2}\) and \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\) gases and \(\mathsf{C H}_{4}/\mathsf{C O}\) selectivity were calculated.
